            "text": "{\n  \"relation\": \"REFINED\",\n  \"consensus_summary\": \"The consensus identifies two bifurcated meanings for the bow: the war-bow symbolizes military power, warfare, and judgment, while the rainbow symbolizes God's covenant and mercy. Correspondingly, breaking the war-bow signifies the cessation of war and the humbling of military might.\",\n  \"book_summary\": \"The book synthesizes these meanings, defining the bow as that which 'strings a people together'—a kingdom's internal covenant, of which its military might is merely the outward face. Consequently, breaking the bow means 'unstringing' or dissolving the kingdom itself.\",\n  \"consensus_core_identification\": \"Distinct referents for military power/warfare (war-bow) and divine promise (rainbow).\",\n  \"book_core_identification\": \"The unifying internal covenant of a people and its outward expression as national might.\",\n  \"core_relation\": \"COMPATIBLE_OVERLAP\",\n  \"extension_relation\": \"PARTIAL_RECLASSIFICATION\",\n  \"boundary_disagreement\": \"The consensus strictly divides the bow into two separate symbolic categories depending on the context (weapon vs. rainbow). The book collapses this boundary, applying the concept of 'covenant' to the human war-bow by arguing that a nation's might is the outward face of its unifying covenant.\",\n  \"material_contradiction\": \"There is no material contradiction; the book embraces the consensus elements of 'weapon', 'might', and 'covenant-token', but reorganizes them. Rather than treating them as separate symbolic tracks, it integrates them into a single structural metaphor based on the imagery of being 'strung together.'\",\n  \"rationale\": \"The book retains the consensus identifications of power and covenant but refines the symbol by merging them. By identifying the human bow as the structural covenant that unites a kingdom, the book refines the act of 'breaking the bow' from mere demilitarization to the deeper dissolution of a kingdom's structural integrity.\",\n  \"citation_support\": \"STRONG\",\n  \"strongest_objection\": \"The synthesis relies on interpreting the shared Hebrew word (qesheth) for weapon and rainbow as evidence of a shared underlying symbolism (covenant). Interpreters may argue that this is a lexical overlap based on visual similarity, and that prophetic texts about breaking a nation's bow (like Elam or Israel) refer strictly to military defeat rather than the 'unstringing' of a national covenant.\",\n  \"confidence\": \"high\"\n}",
